Acute Abdomen
Acute Urgent Care
Severe abdominal pain, Rigidity/Guarding, Rebound tenderness, Free intraperitoneal air

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-001
Reason for Visit: Severe, unrelenting generalized abdominal pain requiring immediate clinical assessment to rule out surgical emergency.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe abdominal pain with associated nausea, tachycardia, and inability to mobilize. Urgent physical assessment was required to evaluate for peritonitis and prevent severe, potentially fatal clinical deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: It feels like my whole stomach is cramped and burning, I cannot even stand up straight or move.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports severe localized and generalized abdominal pain that commenced abruptly 4 hours ago. It began as a dull ache and rapidly evolved into a sharp, constant pain. The patient has experienced concurrent nausea and two episodes of vomiting. Subjective fever is noted. Patient has been lying completely still and reports that taking deep breaths or shifting positions exacerbates the pain significantly, resulting in shallow breathing to minimize abdominal movement.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to pain severity.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapid decompensation; spouse is overwhelmed and anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 112 | Temp: 38.2 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient in obvious distress, lying motionless in fetal position, diaphoretic and flushed.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is non-distended but exhibits marked rigidity and involuntary guarding in all four quadrants. Pronounced rebound tenderness is present globally. Bowel sounds are diminished in all quadrants. Tachycardia present with normal rhythm. Lungs clear to auscultation, though respiratory effort is intentionally shallow. These clinical findings are highly suspicious for a visceral perforation, with free intraperitoneal air expected on an erect chest radiograph.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathway to the exit; well-lit room suitable for stretcher access if needed.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against bedside blister pack; no recent NSAID use to explain sudden ulceration.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Abdomen - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of board-like abdominal rigidity and severe rebound tenderness, which point to peritonitis rather than mucosal inflammation.

Renal Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of flank-to-groin radiation and the fact that the patient is lying completely still, rather than writhing to find a comfortable position.

Lower Respiratory Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to clear lung fields on auscultation and lack of productive cough, despite the presence of shallow breathing.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s presentation of sudden-onset, severe generalized abdominal pain with objective findings of involuntary guarding, rigidity, rebound tenderness, and tachycardia is highly indicative of an acute surgical abdomen, potentially involving viscus perforation. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would expose the patient to an unacceptable risk of profound sepsis, shock, and mortality. The urgency of the home visit was unequivocally justified by the triage history of worsening systemic symptoms and localized peritonitis, necessitating immediate face-to-face evaluation and stabilization to facilitate emergency transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg ODT for nausea. Nil by mouth (NBM) strictly enforced. Immediate escalation to emergency services.
Positive Behavioral Change: By keeping strictly nil by mouth, you prevent further leakage of stomach contents into your abdominal cavity, which directly reduces the risk of worsening infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None applicable; requires emergency transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to emergency department team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the nearest Emergency Department via ambulance (000) for surgical evaluation and diagnostic imaging (erect chest radiograph or abdominal CT).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis is presumptive pending advanced imaging and surgical consultation; the condition is highly dynamic and life-threatening.
Clinical Course: Without surgical intervention, the condition will rapidly progress to systemic sepsis. Prompt surgical exploration typically resolves the underlying defect.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not consume any food or water. Keep completely still to minimize pain. Await ambulance arrival. If consciousness drops or breathing becomes unmanageable, paramedics will intervene immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are keeping your stomach completely empty so the surgical team can operate safely and so nothing further enters your abdominal cavity.
Actionable Steps: Continue to take shallow breaths to control the pain, and remain completely still on your side until the paramedics move you.

